{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Medical triage often uses probabilistic survival estimates (induction), whereas strict Popperism forbids this.","Chess openings are chosen because they have statistically led to wins in the past (inductive), not just because they haven't been refuted."],"overturn_conditions":["If it is demonstrated that avoiding known errors logically entails an expectation of future success.","If no action can be taken without an implicit probability assignment greater than zero for success."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on elimination of known failures rather than prediction of optimal success.","Reliance on past data strictly to identify errors, not to project trends.","Decision rule is negative (avoid X) rather than positive (choose Y because it works)."],"source_memory_refs":["medical_triage_protocol","chess_opening_book"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a doctor might avoid a treatment known to kill (even if unsure what works), a Popperian scientist avoids falsified theories.","The rationality of the choice derives entirely from the avoidance of the known bad outcome, not the anticipation of the good one."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for the better-corroborated theory is defensible without smuggling in induction if framed strictly as a 'critical selection rule' rather than a predictive probability assignment; however, this defense survives only by accepting the cost that rational action becomes a game of avoiding known falsifications rather than maximizing expected success, leaving the agent unable to positively justify why the future will resemble the past even in the absence of known errors.","counterconditions":["If the agent acts on Theory A expecting it to work (e.g., building a bridge), they are implicitly assigning a high subjective probability to its success, which reintroduces induction under the guise of pragmatism.","If the 'risk minimization' strategy assumes that conditions remaining constant enough for past tests to matter implies future stability, it secretly relies on the uniformity of nature.","If Popper's notion of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) is required to explain why we prefer one unfalsified theory over another, and verisimilitude cannot be formally defined without probabilistic assumptions (as shown by Tichý and Miller), the non-inductive defense collapses."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reframe the choice not as 'Theory A is more likely true' (induction) but as 'Theory B has been proven false, Theory A has not yet been proven false; therefore, to minimize immediate error, select A.' This relies on the asymmetry of falsification: while we cannot verify truth, we can identify specific falsehoods. The preference is thus a negative elimination strategy (survival of the least-refuted) rather than a positive confirmation strategy.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}